---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import { LICENSING, PAID_PLUGINS, PLUGIN_NAMES, PRICE_LINE } from '../data/pricing';

const APACHE = 'https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0';
const notice = 'Required Notice: Copyright (c) 2026 Elliott Wave LLC (https://elliottwave.studio)';
const folders: Record<string, string> = { wordpress: 'packages/wordpress', shopify: 'packages/shopify', wix: 'packages/wix', framer: 'packages/framer', hubspot: 'packages/hubspot', react: 'packages/react', vue: 'packages/vue' };
const thirdParty = [
  ['<model-viewer>', SITE.modelViewerVersion, 'Apache License 2.0', 'Copyright Google LLC', 'https://github.com/google/model-viewer'],
  ['three.js', SITE.threeVersion, 'MIT', 'Copyright (c) 2010-2026 three.js authors', 'https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js'],
  ['fflate (in three.js)', '0.8.2', 'MIT', 'Copyright (c) Arjun Barrett', 'https://github.com/101arrowz/fflate'],
  ['Lit', '', 'BSD 3-Clause', 'Copyright (c) 2017 Google LLC', 'https://github.com/lit/lit'],
  ['gainmap-js', '', 'MIT', 'Copyright (c) 2023 MONOGRID', 'https://github.com/MONOGRID/gainmap-js'],
];
---

<Base title="Licence" description={`${SITE.displayName}: the element and snippets are free under Apache-2.0; the platform plugins are paid, per domain.`}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Project</p>
  <h1>Licence</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>{SITE.displayName} has two parts, under two licences. The licensor of both is {SITE.legalEntity}.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Licence</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>The <code>&lt;{SITE.tagName}&gt;</code> element and script, the snippet generator and every snippet it makes (HTML, Squarespace, Webflow, Ghost, Duda and the other paste-in builders, the framework examples), the iframe embed, and the hosting tools</td>
          <td><a href={APACHE}>Apache License 2.0</a></td>
          <td><span class="tag ok">Free</span> including commercial use</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The plugins: {PAID_PLUGINS.map((p) => PLUGIN_NAMES[p]).join(', ')}</td>
          <td>Elliott Wave Plugin Licence</td>
          <td><span class="tag amber">Paid</span> {PRICE_LINE}, per plugin per domain</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>This page is a summary. The licence texts decide; each folder in the <a href={SITE.repoUrl}>source repository</a> has its <code>LICENSE</code> file.</p>

    <h2 id="free">The free parts (Apache-2.0)</h2>
    <p>
      You may use, change and share the element, the snippets and the hosting tools for any purpose, including on business websites and
      online stores, at no cost. If you share copies (changed or not), include the Apache licence and the <code>NOTICE</code> file, and say
      which files you changed. The licence includes a patent grant. There is no warranty.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pasting a snippet into your site, using the iframe embed, or loading the script from jsDelivr needs nothing else: no key, no
      account. The element does no licence check unless a paid plugin asks it to.
    </p>

    <h2 id="plugins">The plugins (paid)</h2>
    <p>
      The plugins are commercial software, sold per plugin and per domain. They are paid for every site, commercial or not. In short, a
      licence lets you:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>install, use and change the plugin on the licensed domain, its www. form, one staging subdomain you name, the platform’s own address for the site (for example your <code>myshopify.com</code> address), and local development hosts;</li>
      <li>use it for any purpose, including client and commercial work, while the licence is valid (yearly: until the end of the paid period; lifetime: no end date);</li>
      <li>move it to another domain twice in any 365 days, and change the staging subdomain three times.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It does not let you share or sell the licence key, use one licence on other domains, remove or get round the licence check, or
      redistribute the plugin except as part of a site you build for a licensed domain. You may install a plugin to try it, and use it on
      local development hosts, without a licence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two plugins on one domain need two licences (for example WordPress and Shopify). The Elementor widget is part of the WordPress
      plugin. The plugin licence is a draft; its terms may change before the plugins go on sale. Prices,
      the buy flow and frequent questions are on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        PAID_PLUGINS.map((p) => (
          <li>
            {PLUGIN_NAMES[p]}: <a href={`${SITE.repoUrl}/blob/main/${folders[p]}/LICENSE`}><code>{folders[p]}/LICENSE</code></a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2 id="checks">Licence checks</h2>
    <p>
      A plugin checks its key with <code>{new URL(LICENSING.api).host}</code>. The WordPress plugin checks from your web server when you
      save the key and about once a week after. The other plugins check in the visitor’s browser, with one cached request per page that
      sends the key and the plugin name, and no cookies or page address. The server stores the purchase email, the licensed domains and,
      for WordPress, a random ID and host for each installation. It stores nothing about visitors.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without a valid licence the plugins keep working: the model shows normally, with a small “Unlicensed” link in the corner, and the
      WordPress plugin shows administrators a notice. If the licence server can’t be reached, nothing changes for 30 days after the last
      successful check.
    </p>

    <h2>Required Notice</h2>
    <p>Both licences ask that copies keep this line:</p>
  </div>
  <Code title="Required Notice" code={notice} />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Third-party software</h2>
    <p>
      The viewer script bundles these libraries. Each stays under its own licence, and their licence texts ship next to every copy of
      <code>{SITE.release.file}</code> and in the <code>NOTICE</code> file. {SITE.displayName} is not made or endorsed by Google.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th>Library</th><th>Version</th><th>Licence</th><th>Copyright</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          thirdParty.map(([name, version, licence, copyright, url]) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={url}>{name}</a></td>
              <td>{version || '—'}</td>
              <td>{licence}</td>
              <td>{copyright}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Marketplaces</h2>
    <p>
      The WordPress plugin is not in the WordPress.org directory, which accepts only GPL-compatible licences. The Shopify, Wix, Framer and
      HubSpot marketplaces sell through their own billing (verify), so the plugins are sold here with a licence key instead, and are not
      listed there yet.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
